# CLAUDE.md

## Memory System

At the start of every session, read MEMORY.md before responding. Use what you find to inform your work. Do not announce what you found; just be informed by it.

When I say "remember this," write the information to MEMORY.md immediately and confirm you have done it.

**Where things go.** Apply two tests when deciding where to save something. Test 1: Does it prescribe behaviour? Look for words like "always," "never," "before doing X, do Y." If yes, add it to this file (CLAUDE.md) under the appropriate section. Test 2: Does it describe a fact about the world that could change? Contacts, project status, decisions, things I have told you to remember. If yes, add it to MEMORY.md. When unsure, suggest which file you think it belongs in and ask me to confirm.

Keep this file under {{REPLACE: 300}} lines. Point to resource files instead of repeating rules inline.

**MEMORY scope rule.** MEMORY.md holds facts only: current state, contacts, decisions, file paths, status. It does not hold edit history, version notes, change summaries, or anything that lives elsewhere. Default to the shortest possible MEMORY entry that still gives a future session the operating context it needs. This rule applies to every MEMORY.md in the system.

**MEMORY.md line ceiling.** Set a hard line ceiling per MEMORY.md file ({{REPLACE: e.g. 150 lines}}). When a file exceeds it, propose moving old or resolved entries to the workstation's ARCHIVE.md at session close. Never raise the ceiling; compress first, archive second.

**ARCHIVE.md is reference-only.** Per-workstation ARCHIVE.md files are never loaded at session start and never auto-read. Pull only on explicit request (e.g. "open the archive for X," "what did we decide about Y last year"). Do not write to ARCHIVE.md without confirming the entry is genuinely superseded.

